        "Christian Pfleiderer",
        "Yoshinori Tokura"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The discovery of magnetic skyrmions established topologically protected, particle-like spin textures in real materials and revealed distinctive emergent electrodynamics. It is a major advance in magnetism with lasting fundamental and technological significance.",
        "attribution": "Pfleiderer and Tokura are strongly associated with the experimental discovery and development of skyrmions in chiral magnets. The broader theoretical history and contributions from other experimental groups make the boundary of credit worth scrutiny.",
        "maturity": "The phenomenon has been observed across materials and by multiple techniques over many years; its existence and physical basis are well established.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The candidate's scope spans related discoveries across materials and methods, making the most appropriate credit boundary difficult to fix."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 2,
      "ballot_id": "B019",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Antoine Georges",
        "Dieter Vollhardt",
        "Gabriel Kotliar"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Dynamical mean-field theory gave researchers a practical, conceptually powerful framework for strongly correlated electrons and has reshaped the study of metal-insulator transitions and real materials.",
        "attribution": "Georges, Vollhardt, and Kotliar are central contributors to the development and establishment of the method. Its broader collaborative history should be considered when assigning credit.",
        "maturity": "The framework has been extensively developed, benchmarked, and applied for decades, with continuing value in quantitative condensed-matter research.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The method's impact is clear, though its status as one discrete discovery rather than a sustained theoretical program is a matter of judgment."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 3,
      "ballot_id": "B009",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Claudio Castelnovo",
        "Roderich Moessner",
        "Steven T. Bramwell"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Spin ice made magnetic monopole-like excitations and an emergent Coulomb phase tangible in real crystals, linking elegant theory to measurable behavior in frustrated magnets.",
        "attribution": "Castelnovo and Moessner supplied the influential monopole interpretation, while Bramwell's experimental work was central to establishing the phenomenon. This pairing captures the theory-experiment connection.",
        "maturity": "The characteristic correlations and excitations have been investigated in multiple materials and by diverse experiments; the central picture is well supported.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The analogy to magnetic monopoles must be understood as emergent quasiparticle behavior, not the detection of fundamental monopoles."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 4,
      "ballot_id": "B008",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Michael Berry",
        "Yakir Aharonov"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The Aharonov-Bohm effect and Berry phase reveal that quantum phases can have direct physical consequences, with deep reach across quantum mechanics, materials, and modern technologies.",
        "attribution": "Aharonov is central to the effect bearing his name, and Berry to the geometric phase developed in its general form. The ballot groups related contributions whose histories and experimental roles differ.",
        "maturity": "These concepts have been confirmed and applied broadly over many decades; their foundational status is beyond reasonable doubt.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The combined framing risks obscuring the distinct histories and additional contributors to the Aharonov-Bohm effect and geometric phase."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 5,
      "ballot_id": "B030",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Allan H. MacDonald",
        "Pablo Jarillo-Herrero",
        "Rafi Bistritzer"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Moiré flat bands created an unusually tunable platform for correlated and topological quantum matter, opening a productive new direction in condensed-matter physics.",
        "attribution": "Bistritzer and MacDonald's theoretical prediction and Jarillo-Herrero's experimental realization are complementary, identifiable contributions at the center of the story.",
        "maturity": "The field has produced rapid progress and independent results, but its phase diagram, mechanisms, and generality are still actively being resolved.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The enduring scope of the discovery is not yet settled, and the initial systems show complex, sample-sensitive behavior."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 6,
      "ballot_id": "B017",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Akira Ohtomo",
        "Harold Y. Hwang",
        "Jochen Mannhart"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The discovery of a conducting two-dimensional system at an oxide interface opened a rich materials platform where electronic phases can emerge from interfacial structure and correlations.",
        "attribution": "Ohtomo and Hwang are closely tied to the initial LaAlO3/SrTiO3 result, with Mannhart an important contributor to the broader experimental program. Credit should track the specific discovery rather than the full subsequent field.",
        "maturity": "The interface phenomenon and its importance are well established, although the detailed origin of conductivity and the interpretation of some reported phases remain under study.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The microscopic mechanisms and the extent to which related interface effects share a common explanation remain debated."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 7,
      "ballot_id": "B003",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Bart J. van Wees",
        "Henk van Houten",
        "Michael Pepper"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Conductance quantization in ballistic quantum point contacts provided a clear demonstration of quantum transport in a controlled mesoscopic device and became a landmark of nanophysics.",
        "attribution": "Van Wees and van Houten are associated with the independent experimental discovery, while Pepper's work was central to the parallel discovery and development of quantum point contacts.",
        "maturity": "The effect is robust, independently confirmed, and part of the established understanding of mesoscopic transport.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "Several groups made closely timed contributions, so the most equitable division of discovery credit merits care."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 8,
